  "subject": "📱 Axios Finish Line: Hit send!",
  "date": "Thu, 17 Sep 2026 21:29:20 -0400",
  "body_text": "96 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n 🏀 Plus: Legends vs. cancer | Thursday, September 17, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n View in browser \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n PRESENTED BY AMERICA’S CREDIT UNIONS \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n Axios Finish Line \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n By Mike Allen and Erica Pandey and Jim VandeHei &middot; Sep 17, 2026 \r\n \r\n \r\n Welcome back! Smart Brevity™ count: 427 words … 1½ mins. Copy edited by Amy Stern. \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 1 big thing: America&#x27;s love language \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios\n\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Texts can't replace in-person hangs or phone calls. But they've dramatically lowered the effort and coordination it takes to show people we care.\n\n 🧮 By the numbers: Texting is the most common way U.S. adults 54 and younger say they've shown someone they care in the past three months, a new Ipsos poll found. \n\n About two-thirds of adults in both the 18–34 and 35–54 age groups said they'd expressed care or appreciation via text. Texting is popular among older adults, too: 59% of those 55 and older said they'd used a text to show someone they care, compared with the 72% who'd done so with a phone call. 💡 Zoom in: The most meaningful text you send might be the random one.\n\n Psychology research shows we routinely underestimate how much our friends — especially those we haven't talked to in a while — would love to hear from us \"just because.\" 🔎 The intrigue: Email and social media are going out of style as connection points, the poll found. \n\n About 1 in 5 adults 54 and younger said they used emails to show care or appreciation. Roughly the same share shouted out their friends and family on social media. Explore the data . \n\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\nA MESSAGE FROM AMERICA’S CREDIT UNIONS\r\n\n \r\n Putting opportunity within reach \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Bringing opportunity to communities often overlooked.\n\n\r\n Since the beginning, credit unions have kept the American Dream within reach for all, sometimes as the only financial institution within a 10-mile radius. Without them, rural and underserved communities would be left behind.\n\n\r\n Learn more. \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n 2. 🏀 Legends vs. cancer \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n Former Old Dominion Monarchs head coach Jeff Jones during a game against the VCU Rams in 2019. Photo: Ryan M. Kelly/Getty Images Since it began in 1993, Coaches vs. Cancer — a collaboration between the American Cancer Society and the National Association of Basketball Coaches — has raised hundreds of millions of dollars for cancer research.\n\n Within that legacy sits the Legends Gala , an annual event in Norfolk, Va., that last year raised close to $415,000. 🥂 The gala is the brainchild of former Old Dominion University and UVA head coach Jeff Jones , who knows firsthand how ugly cancer is. Jones was diagnosed with prostate cancer in 2015, and complications from the cancer and its treatment have persisted, including eight ER visits this year, he told The Virginian-Pilot .\n\n His wife, Axios senior adviser Danielle Decker Jones (DJ), tells us, \"I see what he endures, the daily ravages, and what he still manages to accomplish. And I know that he is a warrior.\" Zoom in: The idea behind the Legends Gala was to bring iconic coaches and athletes together with Coaches vs. Cancer supporters. The success of last year's event earned Coach Jones ACS' prestigious Volunteer Leadership Award .\n\n This year's star lineup includes UConn women's head coach Geno Auriemma, UVA men's head coach Ryan Odom and former LA Chargers quarterback Taylor Heinicke. The gala is tomorrow evening. Donate to the American Cancer Society . \r\n \r\n &nbsp; \r\n \r\n 🏁 Please invite your friends to join Finish Line . \n\n \r\n 👆 Like this comms style and format? It's called Smart Brevity®. Bring it to your org — via hands-on training or internal comms software — to harness its power and impact. \r\n &nbsp; Axios thanks our partners for supporting our journalism.\n Advertise with us. \n\n Read more about Axios' mission , ethics and how we use AI in our journalism . You can reach the authors by replying to this email.\nSponsorship has no influence on editorial content.\n\n Thank you for signing up for this Axios newsletter. To stop receiving this newsletter, unsubscribe or manage your email preferences . If someone forwarded this to you, sign up now to get Axios in your inbox.\n\n Axios, PO Box 101060, Arlington VA 22201 &nbsp; Follow Axios across:\n\n &nbsp; &nbsp; \r\n \r\n \r\n \r\n &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;",
